From 2e8a6c3c771658cd020ec0b7c42972162dd4844c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 06:26:38 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Ambient occlusion and the game's own local lights

Patch 0006 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled, .scaffold/upgrades/lighting-design.md).

Level 2 of the row. Two passes join the shadows, and the direct pass folds all three into the
one factor the composite multiplies the game's picture by. The occlusion pass sends a few cosine
weighted rays over the hemisphere as far as a design constant in world units, each blocked ray
counting more the closer it was stopped, and writes the fraction of ambient light the surface can
see. The local lights pass takes the game's own point lights (a torch, a candle, a fire) at the
game's own falloff, 1 - (d / r)^2, each with a shadow ray toward a point on the flame, and writes
their sum. The direct pass divides the game's lighting with the occlusion on its ambient, the
sun's shadow on its directional term and the local lights added, by the game's lighting as it
was, so a torch's light reaches a surface through the surface's own color and a shadow darkens
to the game's ambient and never below.

The lights come from the program, not the light manager: this game hands the microcode a per
actor directional stand-in for each point light and none at all for the rooms (Lights_BindPoint
in the decompilation), so the light manager never sees one. The program reads the game's light
list once a frame and publishes it to the render queues under their lock; the queue hands it to
the framebuffer renderer every frame, and the traced scene takes it before the projection's own.

The directional light is carried whether or not the frame has a sky; the sky decides only
whether it casts a shadow (a room's directional light is a fill the game invents, and it stays
in the arithmetic as the game's own lighting). Translucent draws (the microcode's XLU z mode)
carry a query mask of their own and no ray sees them: a flame's billboard would otherwise stand
between every surface and its torch.

Pinned edits, each marked "patch 0006": the level, the sample count and the shadow flag in the
parameter block where the padding words were; two visualization modes; the two textures in the
common descriptor set and the shader's register block; the query mask; the lights' path through
the shared queue resources, the workload queue and the framebuffer renderer; the two dispatches
with their timer spans and the barrier before the direct pass; the debug views.

